course: dictionary
lesson: 6804
title: Organised: tidy, planned and ready
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Organised means tidy and well planned, so things are where they should be and get done on time. A pupil who packs their bag the night before is being organised.
explain: Organised can also mean arranged or planned by someone. In «fireworks are best watched at organised displays, where trained people let them off», the display was planned and run by people in charge, so it is safe.
explain: Organise is also a verb. If you organised something, you planned it or put it in order. To check a meaning, swap it into the sentence: if «planned» still makes sense, you have understood the word.
fact: Organised is an adjective: organised, more organised, most organised.
fact: Organise is a verb: organise, organises, organised, organising. Organised is also its past form.
fact: In maths and science, organised data in a table or chart is easy to read.
fact: «Get organised» means start putting things in order so you are ready.
fact: «Organised» can describe a trip or a sport that someone has planned in advance.
exercise: «Fireworks are best watched at organised displays, where trained people let them off.» What does organised mean here? | Loud and noisy | Hidden away from the crowd | Badly made and dangerous | *Planned and run by people in charge
exercise: Maya says, «Be organised: pack your bag the night before.» What does being organised mean? | Shouting loudly at everyone | Forgetting your homework on purpose | *Things are tidy and ready, and done on time | Losing things and being late
exercise: Find and copy the past form of the verb organise in this sentence: «She organised a trip to the museum.» | museum | *organised | she | trip
test: «Our class organised a bake sale for the school garden.» In this sentence, what does organised mean? | Broke | *Planned | Forgot | Hid
test: Which word describes a tidy desk with every pen in its place? | *Organised | Barely | Curt | Glum
exam: Which is the correct past form of organise? | organise | organising | organises | *organised
